anger on the streets of peru's capital  lima protesters are demanding that  spanish oil giant repsol lose its  operating licence after nearly 12 000  barrels of crude oil leaked into the  pacific ocean just off the coast of lima  we are outraged by the pollution and  we're going to continue the nationwide  fight that's why i'm here i want repsol  to leave for all transnational companies  which have only come here to steal from  us to go away  the spill happened while a tanker was  offloading crude oil to a refinery  repsor says the pipeline was damaged by  the tsunami triggered by the recent  volcanic eruption near tonga  but government officials say the ship's  captain doubts that and protesters  accuse repsol of spreading  misinformation  just like thousands of other citizens  we've come to protest against pollution  and against the lies repsol is peddling  it systematically said that the damage  was minimal then said it was as a result  of the tsunami then it told us the  incorrect quantity of oil spilled  the environment minister has banned  repsol from unloading oil until further  notice he also wants guarantees the  company is properly maintaining its oil  pipelines  first we call for a halt to all  hydrocarbon loading and unloading  activities in the peruvian sea by repsol  until it provides us with technical  guarantees that no further damage will  be done to the sea that belongs to all  peruvians  repsol says the decision is  disproportionate and unreasonable it  says it's hired more than 2 000 people  including local fishermen to clean up  the polluted beaches but many peruvians  won't be satisfied until the government  orders repsol to leave  victoria gatenbee al jazeera
 

